Draw the **Lattice transport belt**, an 8-frame **sprite sheet** of an animated
factory conveyor surface, using only the drawing tool.

The belt is a renderer sprite for **Lattice**, a grid-based factory simulation
rendered in a high-angle, pseudo-3D style; the belt is its flat, ground-level
layer, drawn essentially from straight above. You are drawing one tile of a
**straight transport belt that flows to the right (East)**: a dark **single**
continuous metal surface — one belt whose full width carries two items side by side,
not two belts — with side rails, tread blades patterned across the surface, and a
central row of amber chevrons painted on it. The **whole surface — blades and
chevrons together — scrolls rightward** across the eight frames so the belt itself
reads as moving and the animation loops seamlessly. The renderer rotates this one
sprite for the other directions, so you draw the East-facing belt only.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. You
build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a time**, each targeting a frame
with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you issue are recorded to a per-frame
action log, and that log is your submission.

Each frame is its **own separate 32×32 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-rect --frame 0 --x 0 --y 0 --width 32 --height 32 --color "#34383d"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the belt sprite sheet up operation by operation until each
`frames/<index>.png` matches the brief: a dark single continuous belt surface (one
belt whose full width carries two items side by side, not two belts) with side
rails, tread blades across the surface, and a central row of East-pointing amber
chevrons painted on it, the whole surface pattern (blades and chevrons) scrolling
right by the same step each frame so the belt reads as moving, frames 0→7 loop
seamlessly, and the segment tiles horizontally. When you are satisfied, stop: the
recorded per-frame action logs are your finished submission.
